# scoursh image sources - rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.6.8
#
# REQUIRED for `scan.sh image` unless every run passes --source explicitly.
# `sast`, `sca`, `iac`, `dast`, `cloud` and `network` do not read this file at
# all (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 14's required-inputs table).
#
# Copy this file to config/images.conf and edit it.
#
# SCOURSH NEVER PULLS AN IMAGE. lib/http.sh refuses any host absent from
# config/scope.conf and there is no third egress channel (tension 19), so an
# image reaches this scanner the way data/advisories.db does: produced on a
# networked box and handed over as a file. Nothing below is a registry
# reference, a daemon socket or a URL, and no key in this schema can become
# one.
#
#   docker save myapp:v1 -o /srv/images/myapp-v1.tar          # shape A
#   skopeo copy docker://myapp:v1 oci:/srv/images/myapp-oci   # shape B
#
# Both commands run wherever the image already is. scoursh reads the result.
#
# THE ID IS YOURS AND IT MUST BE STABLE. `id` is the `image-id` coverage cell
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5.1): it is what lets a CVE that a rebuild patched
# be classified `fixed` on the next run rather than `unknown` plus `new`. The
# digest changes on every rebuild and the tag changes on every release, so
# either one as the id would put every finding in a fresh cell forever and
# destroy the diff - which is why `id` is never checked against the image's
# own content, deliberately. Name the THING you keep scanning ("the payments
# API image"), not the build you happen to be scanning today.
#
# `path` is a local filesystem path: the docker-save tarball for
# `source: docker-archive`, the OCI image-layout DIRECTORY (the one holding
# `index.json` and `oci-layout`) for `source: oci-layout`. A relative path
# resolves against the working directory the scan runs in, never against the
# install root, so prefer an absolute one.
#
# `reference` picks one image out of a source that holds several - a RepoTag
# for a docker-archive, the org.opencontainers.image.ref.name annotation for
# an OCI layout. A source holding exactly one image needs no `reference`; a
# source holding several and naming none is REFUSED with a recorded reason,
# never resolved by picking the first one, because "scoursh scanned an image
# from this file" and "scoursh scanned the image you meant" are different
# facts and only an operator can tell them apart.
#
# NOTHING BELOW NAMES A REAL IMAGE, REGISTRY OR HOST: every path is an
# illustrative placeholder. scoursh ships no scannable target of its own
# (docs/DESIGN.md §1).

id: payments-api
source: docker-archive
path: /srv/scoursh-images/payments-api.tar
notes: Produced by `docker save payments-api:$TAG -o
  /srv/scoursh-images/payments-api.tar` on the build host and copied here.
  The id stays `payments-api` across every rebuild - that is the whole point
  of it. A pipe | a colon: a hash # and a "quote" are all ordinary bytes in
  this field, which is why config/images.conf uses the block-record format
  rather than a delimited line.

id: payments-worker
source: oci-layout
path: /srv/scoursh-images/payments-worker-oci
reference: payments-worker:staging
notes: An OCI image layout directory (index.json + oci-layout + blobs/), the
  shape `skopeo copy ... oci:DIR` writes. This layout holds more than one
  tagged image, so `reference` names which one to read; without it the run
  refuses rather than picking one.
